  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Drier and warmer Friday with only some scattered \nafternoon showers and a mix of clouds and sun. \n\nFinally some mid level height rises today as weak ridging builds in \nwith building high pressure at the surface. Even so, the trough is \nslow to exit and cyclonic flow with a cold pool aloft (500 mb temps \n-10 to -12C) will, after more sun/clearing in the morning, allow for \ndiurnal cumulus to pop us as well as some widely scattered afternoon \nshowers. That being said, the airmass is drier under backing NW flow \nand most locations will be dry. Regardless, the airmass will be \nwarming allowing highs to get back into the upper 70s and low 80s. \nLight winds mean coastal seabreezes keep the immediate coastline \ncomfortably in the mid 70s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A cold front brings the return of rain Sunday night \nand Monday, perhaps lingering into Tuesday. \n\nAfter drier and warmer (mid to upper 80s) weather on Saturday under \na brief ridge of high pressure the patterns again begins to break \ndown toward the end of the weekend/start of the work week. This is \nalso where we begin to see model consensus decrease, so confidence \nin exact timing details is low. That being said, there is good \nagreement that an upper trough will dig into the east coast pulling \na deep plume of subtropical moisture north ahead of it and into New \nEngland centered around Monday. The GFS is the more amplified \nsolution with a stronger southern cut-off low lingering into next \nweek and higher rainfall totals for SNE. A key component will be the \ncold front which crosses the region on Monday focusing the best \nforcing for ascent coincident with PWATs over 2\". Considering the \nrain some locations have gotten this week (5+ inches in localized \nspots), while it doesn't appear overly concerning WRT flooding it is \nworth watching. There remains a question as to how long rain \nlingers, potentially into Tuesday for some locations. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Drying and warming trend toward Wed/Thu.\n\nWestern Atlantic high pressure then builds west as mid level ridging \nfollows and we'll see a drier and steadily warming period Wed/Thu. \nThis may be a brief respite before more unsettled weather on the \nhorizon.",
